                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "War of the Human Tanks - ALTeR, developed by Yakiniku Banzai and published by Fruitbat Factory, is a fascinating example of an independent strategy game that succeeds through creativity rather than spectacle. Serving as a direct sequel to the original War of the Human Tanks, the game revisits its unusual alternate-history setting where living weapons known as Human Tanks are central to an ongoing conflict. Instead of continuing from the same perspective, ALTeR shifts the narrative focus to the Royal Army, offering players a chance to experience the other side of the war. This change immediately gives the sequel a stronger identity, transforming what could have been a familiar retelling into a story about perspective, sacrifice, and the gray areas that exist in times of conflict.\n\nAt its core, ALTeR blends turn-based tactical combat with visual novel elements. Battles are fought across small, grid-based maps where positioning and resource management are often more important than brute force. Every Human Tank class fulfills a specific battlefield role, encouraging players to experiment with formations and strategies to overcome increasingly difficult encounters. The game's pacing works well for short play sessions, as missions rarely overstay their welcome. However, the strategic depth can occasionally be undermined by abrupt difficulty spikes that require repeated attempts to fully understand enemy patterns and mission objectives. While the controls are more polished than those of its predecessor, some systems still carry the rough edges commonly associated with niche indie releases.\n\nThe narrative is where ALTeR distinguishes itself the most. By presenting former antagonists as protagonists, the game encourages players to reconsider the events of the first title from an entirely different angle. Characters receive more development, and the writing explores themes of duty, nationalism, and the human cost of war without losing the series' signature charm. There is a surprising amount of emotional weight behind the interactions between soldiers and commanders, especially considering the game's simplistic visual style. Fans invested in the series' lore will appreciate how the sequel expands the world and fills in important gaps, while newcomers may find themselves intrigued enough to seek out the original game afterward.\n\nPresentation remains modest but effective. Character artwork carries a distinctive anime-inspired aesthetic that complements the game's tone, and while battlefield environments are simple, they communicate the necessary information clearly. The soundtrack deserves recognition for reinforcing both the tension of combat and the quieter moments between missions. Rather than relying on flashy effects or cinematic sequences, ALTeR uses its limited budget wisely, focusing on atmosphere and storytelling. The result is a game that feels earnest and confident in its identity, even if it lacks the visual appeal expected from larger strategy releases.\n\nThere are still limitations that may prevent the game from reaching a broader audience. Players who prefer action-heavy strategy titles may find the extensive dialogue sequences disruptive, while those unfamiliar with visual novels could struggle with the balance between reading and gameplay. Likewise, some mission designs can feel punishing, particularly when the game expects players to learn through failure. Nevertheless, these shortcomings do little to overshadow the care put into the overall experience. ALTeR never attempts to be something it is not; instead, it embraces its niche and delivers a thoughtful sequel that rewards patience and attention.\n\nUltimately, War of the Human Tanks - ALTeR is a compelling continuation of a unique franchise. Its willingness to challenge players' assumptions, coupled with satisfying tactical gameplay and an engaging storyline, makes it an easy recommendation for fans of indie strategy games and visual novels. It may not possess the production values of major releases, but its creativity and heartfelt execution leave a lasting impression. Few games manage to make players sympathize with both sides of a conflict as effectively as ALTeR, and that accomplishment alone makes it worthy of recognition.\n\nRating: 8/10",
